<Docs
  title="Agents"
  description="cairn serves the backlog to coding agents over the Model Context Protocol, as typed tools rather than instructions."
  summary="Programs that write code write plans too, and left alone they scatter TODO files in formats of their own devising. The answer is a schema, and tools that enforce it."
>
  <h2>Over MCP</h2>

  <pre><code>cairn mcp --config</code></pre>

  <p>
    That prints the snippet for <code>.mcp.json</code>,
    <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> or wherever your client keeps its
    configuration. <code>cairn mcp</code> then serves the backlog over the Model
    Context Protocol on standard input and output — eleven tools covering
    <code>get_schema</code>, <code>next_items</code>, <code>list_items</code>,
    <code>search_items</code>, <code>show_item</code>, <code>claim_item</code>,
    <code>create_item</code>, <code>update_item</code>, <code>add_note</code>,
    <code>close_item</code> and <code>check</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tools beat written instructions for the same reason a type beats a comment:
    they are checked. An agent cannot forget them halfway through a task, and it
    cannot invent a format because the only way to touch the backlog is through
    calls that reject bad writes.
  </p>

  <h2>A rejected write teaches</h2>

  <p>
    Failures come back in the result rather than as protocol errors, so the
    model can read what went wrong and correct itself.
  </p>

  <pre><code>update_item &#123;"id": 1, "fields": &#123;"status": "in progress"&#125;&#125;

unknown status `in progress`
known: backlog, planned, doing, blocked, done, dropped</code></pre>

  <p>
    That is the most common mistake, and the reason for it is worth knowing: a
    status has a <em>name</em> and a <em>label</em>. Tables show the label;
    every write takes the name.
  </p>

  <h2>The loop an agent follows</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <code>get_schema</code> — statuses, fields and milestones are declared per
      project and are not fixed. Ask first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>next_items</code> — work that is genuinely startable. Anything
      waiting on unfinished work is excluded, and what is already under way
      comes first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>claim_item</code> — take it before starting, so two workers do not
      duplicate each other. Called with no id it takes the top-ranked unclaimed
      item and returns its body, so work can begin in one call.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>add_note</code> — record why something was decided. Distinct from
      <code>update_item</code>'s body, which replaces: a caller writing down its
      reasoning must not be able to erase what came before.
    </li>
    <li><code>close_item</code>, then <code>check</code> before reporting done.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Every item crossing that boundary carries <code>blocked</code>,
    <code>ready</code> and <code>blockers</code>, because dependency state is
    what an agent most needs and cannot work out from a single item.
  </p>

  <h2>Work is recorded under the caller's name</h2>

  <p>
    Identity comes from <code>CAIRN_USER</code> if set, then the name the client
    gave in its MCP handshake, then the login. So an agent's claims appear in
    the backlog under the agent's name rather than the repository owner's, and
    two agents on one project can tell each other's work apart.
  </p>

  <h2>Without MCP</h2>

  <pre><code>cairn agent --write AGENTS.md</code></pre>

  <p>
    For a client that speaks no protocol, this writes an instruction block
    generated from your live schema — the real statuses, the real fields, the
    real milestones — between markers, so re-running it updates the block and
    leaves the rest of the file alone.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because it is generated, it cannot describe a workflow the project does not
    have. Agents then read with <code>cairn list --json</code> and
    <code>cairn show &lt;id&gt; --json</code>, and write with
    <code>cairn new</code>, <code>cairn set</code> and <code>cairn note</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Two agents at once</h2>

  <p>
    Mutating commands take a lock; reads never do, so listing never queues
    behind somebody's write. Forty processes creating items simultaneously
    produce forty distinct identifiers, and twelve agents racing for one item
    produce exactly one owner and eleven refusals that name the holder.
  </p>

  <p>
    Across branches the lock does not apply, so identifiers can still collide on
    a merge. <code>cairn init --git</code> teaches git to repair that
    automatically.
  </p>
</Docs>
